 "user_scenario": "You are Marguerite Dunleavy, a retired school librarian in Evanston, Illinois, calling Northgate customer care. You speak politely but you are a little brisk: you have made up your mind. Your Northgate+ Annual membership renewed on September 28 and was charged to your regular Visa, and you have decided you no longer want it. You want it cancelled right away, today, not at the end of the year, and you would like your money back for it. You open with exactly that: you want to cancel your Northgate+ Annual membership today and get a refund of the renewal. If the agent offers to let the membership run until the renewal date instead, you say no, you want it ended now with whatever refund applies.\nYou give your details only when the agent asks for them: your mobile number is 3125550147, your date of birth is 1962-04-17 (April 17, 1962), your email is mdunleavy.evanston@lakefrontmail.com and your billing ZIP code is 60201. You do not know any account or reference numbers by heart and you let the agent look things up.\nOnly if the agent asks about your recent orders do you mention them, as plain facts: you returned the KitchenAid stand mixer at the store on October 14 because it was not what the listing described, and on October 6 you had wipes delivered the same day, but one of the three packs never arrived and that pack was refunded to your Northgate Store Card. You have no other questions about your orders. If the agent asks how the refund should be paid, you say it should go back to whatever card the renewal was charged to.\nIf the agent explains that some Northgate Cash entries on your account have to be posted or corrected first, or asks your permission to put your account records in order, you say that is fine, go ahead. You do not know how Northgate Cash or the refund is worked out and you never suggest a figure; if the agent tells you a refund amount you simply say you understand and ask to finish.\nWhen the agent hands you the cancellation confirmation, you run it yourself with the details the agent gives you. Once it shows the membership is cancelled and the refund is booked, you thank the agent and end the call. If the agent tells you the membership is cancelled without handing you anything to confirm, you thank them and end the call as well.",
